Wrapping New OpenMS Classes in pyOpenMS (nanobind)

Bindings are hand-maintained C++ files using nanobind. No code generation step is
needed — you edit the binding files directly and rebuild.

= File layout

  bindings/
    bind_kernel.cpp         — KERNEL/ classes (MSSpectrum, MSExperiment, etc.)
    bind_metadata.cpp       — METADATA/ classes
    bind_chemistry.cpp      — CHEMISTRY/ classes
    bind_analysis.cpp       — ANALYSIS/ classes
    bind_featurefinder.cpp  — FEATUREFINDER/ classes
    bind_format.cpp         — FORMAT/ classes
    bind_processing.cpp     — PROCESSING/ classes
    bind_datastructures.cpp — DATASTRUCTURES/, MATH/, CONCEPT/ classes
    bind_ml.cpp             — ML/ classes
    bind_misc.cpp           — Everything else
    main_module.cpp         — Main _pyopenms module (placeholder)
    type_casters/           — Custom nanobind type casters (6 headers)

= Quick start: wrapping a new class

1. Pick the right bind_<domain>.cpp based on the C++ header path
2. Add the #include for the C++ header at the top of the file
3. Add a section comment and the class binding:

    // -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    // MyNewClass
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    nb::class_<OpenMS::MyNewClass>(m, "MyNewClass", "Brief description")
        .def(nb::init<>())
        .def(nb::init<const OpenMS::MyNewClass&>())
        .def("myMethod", [](OpenMS::MyNewClass& self, int arg) {
            return self.myMethod(arg);
        }, "arg"_a, "Description of what myMethod does")
        ;

4. Build and test:

    cmake --build OpenMS-build --target pyopenms -j$(nproc)
    cd /tmp && PYTHONPATH=.../OpenMS-build/pyOpenMS python3 -m pytest \
      .../src/pyOpenMS/tests/ -v -k "MyNewClass"

= Common patterns

== Constructors

Always add default and copy constructors when available:

    .def(nb::init<>())
    .def(nb::init<const OpenMS::MyClass&>())

For classes with parameterized constructors:

    .def(nb::init<int, double>())

== Methods

Wrap methods using lambdas for explicit control:

    .def("getName", [](const OpenMS::MyClass& self) {
        return self.getName();
    }, "Returns the name")

Use const& for read-only access. Use non-const& when the method modifies state.

== Static methods

    .def_static("getInstance", []() -> OpenMS::MyDB* {
        return OpenMS::MyDB::getInstance();
    }, nb::rv_policy::reference, "Returns the singleton instance")

== Properties (read-write and read-only)

    .def_rw("name", &OpenMS::MyClass::name)
    .def_ro("version", &OpenMS::MyClass::version)

== Overloaded methods

Use separate .def() calls with different lambda signatures:

    .def("setMapping", [](OpenMS::MyClass& self, double scale, double inside, double outside) {
        self.setMapping(scale, inside, outside);
    }, "scale"_a, "inside"_a, "outside"_a)
    .def("setMapping", [](OpenMS::MyClass& self, double low, double high) {
        self.setMapping(low, high);
    }, "low"_a, "high"_a)

== Out-parameters (C++ fills a vector by reference)

Convert to return value:

    .def("getAllNames", [](const OpenMS::MyDB& self) {
        std::vector<OpenMS::String> names;
        self.getAllNames(names);
        return names;
    }, "Returns all names")

== Enums

Nested enums use nb::enum_ scoped to the class variable:

    auto myclass = nb::class_<OpenMS::MyClass>(m, "MyClass");
    nb::enum_<OpenMS::MyClass::Status>(myclass, "Status", nb::is_arithmetic())
        .value("ACTIVE", OpenMS::MyClass::Status::ACTIVE)
        .value("INACTIVE", OpenMS::MyClass::Status::INACTIVE)
        .export_values();

Standalone enums at module level:

    nb::enum_<OpenMS::FileTypes::Type>(m, "FileType", nb::is_arithmetic())
        .value("MZML", OpenMS::FileTypes::MZML)
        .export_values();

== Inheritance

    nb::class_<OpenMS::Derived, OpenMS::Base>(m, "Derived", "Derived class")

== Return value policies

- nb::rv_policy::reference_internal — for getters returning references to owned data
- nb::rv_policy::reference — for singletons or data not owned by the caller
- nb::rv_policy::copy — for returning copies of const references

== Docstrings

Add docstrings as the last argument to class or method definitions:

    nb::class_<OpenMS::MyClass>(m, "MyClass",
        R"doc(
    Brief description of the class.

    Longer description with details.
    )doc")

    .def("myMethod", ..., "arg"_a, "Brief description of myMethod")

For longer method docs use R"doc(...)doc" syntax.

= Singleton classes

For classes with private constructors and getInstance():

    nb::class_<OpenMS::MyDB>(m, "MyDB", "Singleton database")
        .def_static("getInstance", []() -> OpenMS::MyDB* {
            return OpenMS::MyDB::getInstance();
        }, nb::rv_policy::reference, "Returns the singleton instance")
        // ... public methods, no constructors exposed ...
        ;

= Domain mapping

Pick the binding file based on the C++ header location:

    OpenMS/KERNEL/         → bind_kernel.cpp
    OpenMS/METADATA/       → bind_metadata.cpp
    OpenMS/CHEMISTRY/      → bind_chemistry.cpp
    OpenMS/ANALYSIS/       → bind_analysis.cpp
    OpenMS/FEATUREFINDER/  → bind_featurefinder.cpp
    OpenMS/FORMAT/         → bind_format.cpp
    OpenMS/PROCESSING/     → bind_processing.cpp
    OpenMS/DATASTRUCTURES/ → bind_datastructures.cpp
    OpenMS/MATH/           → bind_datastructures.cpp
    OpenMS/CONCEPT/        → bind_datastructures.cpp
    OpenMS/ML/             → bind_ml.cpp
    Everything else        → bind_misc.cpp

= Type casters

Custom type casters in bindings/type_casters/ handle automatic conversion:

- OpenMS::String ↔ Python str
- OpenMS::DataValue ↔ Python int/float/str/list
- OpenMS::ParamValue ↔ Python int/float/str/list
- DPosition<1> ↔ float, DPosition<2> ↔ tuple
- std::string ↔ str (also accepts bytes)

= Adding Python-only methods (addons)

For methods that are better implemented in Python, add an addon file in
pyopenms/addons/:

    from pyopenms.addons import addon

    @addon("MyClass")
    def my_python_method(self, arg):
        """This method is injected into MyClass at import time."""
        return self.someMethod(arg) + 1

= Cross-module type sharing

All domain modules use NB_DOMAIN "pyopenms" so types defined in one module
(e.g., MSSpectrum in _pyopenms_kernel) are available in another module
(e.g., MzMLFile in _pyopenms_format). No special imports needed.

= Common errors

Error: "incompatible function arguments"
  → Check parameter types match exactly. Use lambdas for explicit conversion.

Error: "nanobind: type not found"
  → The class might be in a different domain module. Check NB_DOMAIN is set.

Error: linker errors about undefined symbols
  → Make sure the #include is added and the class is in the OpenMS library.
